theory CompliantCeremony
begin

/*
================================================================================
  WITNESS NOMINATION -- what an honest participant refuses to sign
================================================================================

  WHICH TREE THIS IS.  `compliant/`.  The obligation modelled here is one the
  design says in terms cannot be checked by anyone else, and names the party
  who can:

    `light-client-requirements.md` -- "Refuse to sign a record attributing to
      you a witness you did not nominate.  Your client knows exactly which
      witnesses it nominated; an entry claiming `nominated_by = you` outside
      that set is the counterparty dressing its own curation in your name, and
      it defeats the split check from inside an apparently balanced record.
      THE WIRE CANNOT CHECK THIS (`nominated_by` is a recorded claim) -- YOU
      CAN, AND YOU ARE THE ONLY PARTY [who can]."

  So `wire-only/ceremony.spthy` structurally cannot hold this property, and
  not through any shortcoming of that theory: `nominated_by` is a claim in a
  record, and a well-formed record can carry a false one.  The check lives in
  one participant's own memory of what it nominated.  This theory is that
  memory made into a state.

  WHAT IT DOES NOT TOUCH.  Co-presence.  That is an axiom in the wire theory
  and remains one here; nothing about nomination bears on whether anybody was
  in a room.  This theory takes the ceremony as happening and asks only whether
  the roster an honest participant signs can misattribute its own nominees.
*/

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  NOMINATION FROM ONE'S OWN, AND THE COUNTERPARTY'S PRUNE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE DIRECTIONAL RULE IS GONE, AND ITS REVERSAL IS THE POINT [author,
  2026-10-19].  This theory used to prove design 7.1's "you nominate from the
  OTHER party's neighbourhood, never your own", with a restriction standing
  for the check a client ran against its own neighbour list.  The design now
  says the opposite: each participant engages up to eight witnesses drawn
  from ITS OWN contacts, before the counterparty has accepted anything,
  because what a witness is for is observing the live transaction rather than
  being adversarially chosen -- "a witness who only observes static records
  doesn't add any friction to a replay attack".  The two lemmas that proved
  the old direction are therefore deleted rather than weakened; a lemma that
  proves a rule the design has reversed is worse than no lemma.

  WHAT REPLACES IT, AND IS MODELLED HERE.  The counterparty's prune.  Each
  side offers its nominees by locator and the other keeps min(3, offered),
  so every surviving witness is NOMINATED BY ONE SIDE AND SELECTED BY THE
  OTHER (design 7.1 item 4, 7.1.1).  That is the structural property the
  split now rests on, and it is what this theory proves: a conforming
  signature over a witness attributed to a party implies both that the party
  nominated it and that the counterparty kept it.

  WHAT THE CONFORMING RULE CHECKS, AND HOW.  `!Nomination` is the client's own
  memory of what it nominated and `!Survivor` is that memory intersected with
  the keep it received -- which is exactly what the obligation names: "your
  client knows exactly which witnesses it nominated and which survived the
  counterparty's prune".  Both are persistent, since consulting a memory does
  not consume it.  The prune is the counterparty's act and so is a rule of its
  own, carrying the inequality that a party does not prune its own set.

  WHAT THIS IS NOT.  It is not the theorem smuggled in as an axiom.  What
  makes the pair say something is `Sign_Without_Checking`, the client that
  consults neither memory, from which both a misattributed nominee and an
  unkept one are reachable -- so the lemmas hold because of the checks and
  not because the model cannot write the failures down.
*/

rule Neighbour:
    [ ]
  --[ IsNeighbour($X, $W) ]->
    [ !InNeighbourhood($X, $W) ]

/*  A participant nominates from ITS OWN contacts [author, 2026-10-19]: the
    acting subnet's horizon, the parties it has met anywhere, and whatever
    else it chose to widen to.  There is no eligibility check left to skip
    here -- 7.1.1 makes even a scraped contact nominable, so that the
    appearance of a party in a witness set is not evidence of membership of
    anybody's subnet -- which is why the rule has no non-conforming twin.  */
rule Nominate:
    [ !InNeighbourhood($P, $W) ]
  --[ Nominated($P, $Other, $W) ]->
    [ !Nomination($P, $Other, $W) ]

/*  THE PRUNE, which is the counterparty's and not the nominator's: it keeps
    some of what the other side offered and dismisses the rest, and what
    survives is nominated by one side and selected by the other.  The count
    (three of eight) is a bound this theory does not need: the property is
    the crossing of the two acts, not the arithmetic, and 7.1.1 carries the
    arithmetic.  Neq says a party does not prune its own set.             */
rule Keep:
    [ !Nomination($P, $Other, $W) ]
  --[ Kept($Other, $P, $W), Neq($P, $Other) ]->
    [ !Survivor($P, $Other, $W) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE RECORD, AND THE SIGNATURE DECISION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A proposed record attributes each witness to a nominator.  The attribution
  is a CLAIM -- `In(...)`, straight off the network, chosen by whoever
  assembled the record.  That is faithful: the counterparty assembles a body
  and offers it, and the wire has no way to tell a true `nominated_by` from a
  false one.

  The honest participant's rule is the guard on `Sign`.  It signs only if its
  own memory of what it nominated agrees with what the record says it
  nominated.  `!Nomination` is persistent because a client's record of its own
  nominations is not consumed by consulting it.
*/

/*  TWO WITNESSES ATTRIBUTED TO $P, not one.  The wire allows sixteen per
    record and requires them distinct; one is enough to state the obligation
    and not enough to represent the way it is usually broken -- check the
    first attribution, sign the whole body.  With one witness there is no
    unchecked second attribution for a checked first one to sit beside, so a
    signer that validated one element and authorised the collection was
    indistinguishable here from one that validated all of them.  A
    cross-family review asked for two.  The record is still a projection:
    the counterparty's own attributions are not carried, only the two the
    body says are $P's.                                                    */
rule Propose_Record:
    [ Fr(~rec), In(<$W1, $W2, $P, $Other>) ]
  --[ Proposed(~rec, $W1, $W2, $P, $Other), Neq($W1, $W2) ]->
    [ !Record(~rec, $W1, $W2, $P, $Other) ]

restriction Inequality:
  "All x #i. Neq(x, x) @ #i ==> F"

/*  THE OBLIGATION AS A GUARD.  Signing consumes nothing and asserts one
    thing per attribution: that witness survived $P's own nomination and the
    counterparty's keep.  BOTH premises, because the check is per attributed
    witness; a record attributing to $P any witness $P never nominated, or
    one the counterparty never kept, cannot reach this rule.  MUTATION
    (run-all.sh 3c): make the second premise check $W1 again -- the signer
    that validates one element and authorises the body -- and
    no_signature_over_a_misattributed_nominee falsifies for $W2.          */
rule Sign_Record:
    [ !Record(rec, $W1, $W2, $P, $Other)
    , !Survivor($P, $Other, $W1)
    , !Survivor($P, $Other, $W2) ]
  --[ Signed(rec, $P)
    , SignedAttributing(rec, $P, $W1), SignedAttributing(rec, $P, $W2) ]->
    [ Out(<'signed', rec, $P>) ]

/*  The dishonest alternative, present so the property is not true merely
    because no rule could ever misattribute.  A client that skips the check
    signs whatever it is handed -- and this is what the wire permits, since
    such a record is well formed.  The lemmas below carve it out by name,
    which is the honest way to state a compliance property: it holds of a
    node that follows the rule, and says nothing about one that does not.  */
rule Sign_Without_Checking:
    [ !Record(rec, $W1, $W2, $P, $Other) ]
  --[ Signed(rec, $P)
    , SignedAttributing(rec, $P, $W1), SignedAttributing(rec, $P, $W2)
    , SkippedTheCheck($P) ]->
    [ Out(<'signed', rec, $P>) ]

/*
================================================================================
  PROPERTIES
================================================================================
*/

/*  THE OBLIGATION.  A participant that ran the check signs no record
    attributing to it a witness it did not nominate.  The carve-out names the
    non-compliant rule rather than a compromise: this is a theory about
    keeping a promise, and the way to break it is to not keep it.          */
lemma no_signature_over_a_misattributed_nominee:
  all-traces
  "All rec P W #i.
      SignedAttributing(rec, P, W) @ #i
      & not(Ex #s. SkippedTheCheck(P) @ #s)
        ==> Ex Other #n. Nominated(P, Other, W) @ #n & #n < #i"

/*  THE CUT AND CHOOSE.  A witness a conforming participant signed for was
    nominated by that participant and KEPT BY THE COUNTERPARTY -- design
    7.1.1's "nominated by one side and selected by the other", which is what
    makes the split mean anything now that the pool is each party's own
    (7.1 item 4).  The carve-out names the participant, as the other lemmas
    here do, and the order says the acts happened in the order the ceremony
    puts them: nominated, then kept, then signed over.                    */
lemma a_signed_witness_was_nominated_by_one_side_and_kept_by_the_other:
  all-traces
  "All rec P W #i.
      SignedAttributing(rec, P, W) @ #i
      & not(Ex #s. SkippedTheCheck(P) @ #s)
        ==> Ex Other #n #k.
              Nominated(P, Other, W) @ #n
            & Kept(Other, P, W) @ #k
            & #n < #k & #k < #i"

/*  AND THE WITNESS THAT THE CHECK IS WHAT HOLDS IT.  Skip it and a nominee
    the counterparty never kept is signed for anyway -- the party that
    offered eight and signed over all eight, whatever the prune said.  If
    this ever falsifies, the lemma above is true because the model cannot
    express the failure, which is where this file started.                */
lemma an_unkept_nominee_is_signed_without_the_check:
  exists-trace
  "Ex rec P Other W #i #n.
      SignedAttributing(rec, P, W) @ #i
    & Nominated(P, Other, W) @ #n
    & SkippedTheCheck(P) @ #i
    & not(Ex O2 #k. Kept(O2, P, W) @ #k)"

/*  ANTI-VACUITY.  An honest participant signs a record.                   */
lemma an_honest_signature_is_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex rec P W #i.
      SignedAttributing(rec, P, W) @ #i
    & not(Ex #s. SkippedTheCheck(P) @ #s)"

/*  THE WITNESS THAT THE CHECK IS LOAD-BEARING.  A misattributed record CAN
    be signed -- by a client that skipped the check.  If this ever falsifies,
    the model has made the wire enforce something the design says it cannot,
    and the first lemma would be true for the wrong reason.                */
lemma misattribution_is_possible_without_the_check:
  exists-trace
  "Ex rec P W #i.
      SignedAttributing(rec, P, W) @ #i
    & SkippedTheCheck(P) @ #i
    & not(Ex Other #n. Nominated(P, Other, W) @ #n)"


/*
================================================================================
  CO-PRESENCE -- what a conforming participant will not sign
================================================================================

  WHY THIS IS HERE AND NOT IN `wire-only/`.  These four properties used to
  live in `wire-only/ceremony.spthy`, and a review was right that they did not
  belong: they hold only of participants who follow the signing discipline,
  and the wire cannot check that anyone did.  design 7 is explicit --
  "bilateral collusion is unpreventable" -- and design 15.4 that presence "is
  not an unforgeable primitive, and bilateral collusion defeats it
  regardless".

  The tell was that the old model let a `CoPresent` token gate the USE OF A
  PARTY'S OWN KEY.  Nothing in the protocol does that, and nothing could.
  Add a rule letting a legitimate principal sign a body it never met for --
  no compromise, its own key -- and all four lemmas falsify at once, which is
  how the partition was settled rather than argued.

  KEY COMPROMISE AND DISHONESTY ARE DIFFERENT CAPABILITIES, and conflating
  them was the actual error.  `Compromised(P)` means someone stole P's key.
  It cannot stand for "P used P's own key contrary to client policy" -- that
  needs no theft, and carving it out under the same name made the theorem
  look stronger than it was.  Here the carve-out is `SkippedTheMeeting`,
  named for what it is.
*/

rule Party:
    [ ] --[ IsParty($P) ]-> [ !Party($P) ]

rule Meeting:
    [ Fr(~cid) ]
  --[ MeetHappened($P1, $P2, ~cid) ]->
    [ CoPresent($P1, ~cid), CoPresent($P2, ~cid), !Roster($P1, $P2, ~cid) ]

/*  THE DISCIPLINE.  A conforming participant signs a presence body only for
    a meeting it was at: the token is consumed, so one meeting licenses one
    signature.                                                              */
rule Sign_Presence_Compliant:
    [ CoPresent($P, cid), !Roster($P1, $P2, cid) ]
  --[ PresenceSigned($P, $P1, $P2, cid) ]->
    [ Sig($P, $P1, $P2, cid) ]

/*  AND THE PARTY THAT DOES NOT KEEP IT.  Its own key, a body off the
    network, no meeting.  This is not an attack on the protocol -- it is two
    people deciding to lie, and the design prices it as attack cost rather
    than preventing it.                                                     */
rule Sign_Presence_Without_Meeting:
    [ !Party($P), In(<$P1, $P2, cid>) ]
  --[ PresenceSigned($P, $P1, $P2, cid), SkippedTheMeeting($P) ]->
    [ Sig($P, $P1, $P2, cid) ]

rule Accept_Presence:
    [ Sig($P1, $P1, $P2, cid), Sig($P2, $P1, $P2, cid) ]
  --[ PresenceAccepted($P1, $P2, cid) ]->
    [ ]

/*  THE CONFORMANCE THEOREM.  Among participants who kept the discipline, an
    accepted record implies the meeting happened.  This is the honest form of
    what `presence_requires_copresence` was reaching for.                   */
/*  THE CARVE-OUT IS THE PARTICIPANTS', NOT THE TRACE'S.  Until 2026-09-08 it
    read `not(Ex P #s. SkippedTheMeeting(P) @ #s)` -- nobody anywhere ever
    skipped -- so an unrelated party fabricating a record elsewhere made the
    theorem say nothing about P1 and P2's.  A cross-family review put it
    exactly: that proves "if every actor conforms then ...", where the
    requirement is a commitment each client makes for itself.  Scoped to the
    two parties the record names, it is the stronger statement and it holds:
    a conforming P1 and P2 keep their obligation beside any number of liars.
    The nomination lemma above had this shape from the start.              */
lemma a_record_between_conforming_participants_implies_a_meeting:
  all-traces
  "All P1 P2 cid #i.
      PresenceAccepted(P1, P2, cid) @ #i
      & not(Ex #s. SkippedTheMeeting(P1) @ #s)
      & not(Ex #s. SkippedTheMeeting(P2) @ #s)
        ==> Ex #m. MeetHappened(P1, P2, cid) @ #m & #m < #i"

/*  ONE MEETING, ONE ROSTER: a conforming participant's signature cannot be
    transplanted onto a different pairing, because the token it consumed
    belonged to that roster.  The honest form of
    `copresence_binds_one_roster`.                                          */
lemma a_conforming_signature_binds_one_roster:
  all-traces
  "All P1 P2 cid Q1 Q2 #i #j.
      PresenceAccepted(P1, P2, cid) @ #i & PresenceAccepted(Q1, Q2, cid) @ #j
      & not(Ex #s. SkippedTheMeeting(P1) @ #s)
      & not(Ex #s. SkippedTheMeeting(P2) @ #s)
      & not(Ex #s. SkippedTheMeeting(Q1) @ #s)
      & not(Ex #s. SkippedTheMeeting(Q2) @ #s)
        ==> P1 = Q1 & P2 = Q2"

/*  ANTI-VACUITY: conforming participants do produce records.               */
lemma a_conforming_record_is_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex P1 P2 cid #i.
      PresenceAccepted(P1, P2, cid) @ #i
    & not(Ex #s. SkippedTheMeeting(P1) @ #s)
    & not(Ex #s. SkippedTheMeeting(P2) @ #s)"

/*  THE RESIDUAL, KEPT REACHABLE.  Two parties who simply lie produce an
    accepted record with no meeting behind it, using nothing but their own
    keys.  design 7 says this is unpreventable, so a model in which it became
    unreachable would be claiming more than the design does -- and would put
    these lemmas back in the wrong tree.                                    */
lemma collusion_produces_a_record_without_a_meeting:
  exists-trace
  "Ex P1 P2 cid #i.
      PresenceAccepted(P1, P2, cid) @ #i
    & not(P1 = P2)                     // wire: the two participant identities
                                       //   MUST differ -- a witness with one
                                       //   party in both slots would not be
                                       //   the record the design accepts
    & not(Ex #m. MeetHappened(P1, P2, cid) @ #m)"


end
